    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`). Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nA structured address form (`addressLine1` + optional `addressLine2`, alongside `city`/`postcode`), preferred over the flat `address` when supplied, with the flat form kept for backward compatibility.\n\n### Changes by responsibility\n\n**API contract \u2014 `src/main/resources/openapi.yml`**\n- Added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` properties to `OwnerFields` (so they appear on both the input DTO and the `Owner` output).\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list (an owner may now supply its address via either form; the cross-field rule lives in the validator). Documented `address` as the composed, normalized read value.\n\n**Domain model \u2014 `Owner.java`**\n- Added persisted `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` columns with getters/setters (MapStruct maps them by name automatically \u2014 no mapper edit needed).\n\n**Persistence \u2014 `db/{hsqldb,h2,postgres,mysql}/schema.sql`**\n- Added `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns to the `owners` table (hsqldb is what the test suite uses; the others updated for parity).\n\n**Validation & normalization \u2014 `OwnerFieldsValidator.java`**\n- New `normalizeAddress`: when `addressLine1` is non-blank, it normalizes both lines (delegating to the existing `AddressNormalizer`), writes them back, and composes `address` as `normalized addressLine1` + a single space + `normalized addressLine2` (appended only when present). Otherwise it normalizes the flat `address` as before.\n- Rejects the create when neither form yields an address; `city` (and the other text fields) stay required.\n\n**Update path \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.updateOwner`**\n- Carries the normalized structured fields onto the persisted owner alongside the composed `address`.\n\n### On the \"reads the address\" consumers\nThe household hash (`HouseholdMatcher`), postcode validation (`PostcodeValidator`) and locality (`Owner.getLocality`/`getRegion`) all read `city`/`postcode`, which are top-level fields shared by both forms \u2014 so they already use the structured values when present and fall back for flat input with no code change. Duplicate/identity detection keys off telephone/email/householdId, so composing `address` doesn't perturb it.\n\nFull suite: `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 green.",
